WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.300
We built each piece on its own.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.600
Schedules.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.900
Locks.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.200
Write-ahead logs.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.500
Recovery.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.800
Now look at how they coordinate.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.100
Five components sit inside one transaction manager, and each one owns an ACID guarantee.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.400
The scheduler interleaves operations into a conflict-serializable order.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.699
That is consistency.

00:00:31.699 --> 00:00:34.999
The lock manager enforces two-phase locking on the tuples transactions share, with O of one hash lookups.

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:38.299
That is isolation.

00:00:38.299 --> 00:00:41.599
The WAL logs every change before the data page moves.

00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:44.899
That is durability.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:48.199
The deadlock detector watches the waits-for graph and aborts a victim when it finds a cycle.

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:51.499
The recovery manager runs analysis, redo, and undo after a crash.

00:00:51.499 --> 00:00:54.799
That is atomicity.

00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:58.099
Five subsystems, and together they turn many concurrent transactions into one consistent state.

00:00:58.099 --> 00:01:01.246
Now watch them run together.

00:01:01.246 --> 00:01:04.394
Mickey buys Premium.

00:01:04.394 --> 00:01:07.541
Minnie updates her profile.

00:01:07.541 --> 00:01:10.689
Daffy adds a song.

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:13.836
Three transactions arriving at the same instant, and they touch different tuples.

00:01:13.836 --> 00:01:16.983
The scheduler routes each one to its own thread.

00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:20.131
The lock manager grants all three locks at once, because the rows do not overlap, so no one waits.

00:01:20.131 --> 00:01:23.278
The WAL logs every change before its commit becomes durable.

00:01:23.278 --> 00:01:26.426
So far so good.

00:01:26.426 --> 00:01:29.573
Five components are wired in.

00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:32.721
On a clean run like this one, how many actually fire?

00:01:32.721 --> 00:01:35.868
Four.

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:39.015
The deadlock detector stays quiet on this trace, because there is no waits-for cycle to break.

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:42.163
The recovery manager stands by, ready only if the server crashes.

00:01:42.163 --> 00:01:45.310
The result is one consistent committed state.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.458
Five components, four firing.

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:51.605
ACID preserved.

00:01:51.605 --> 00:01:56.093
Why does this scale?

00:01:56.093 --> 00:02:00.581
Because most transactions never touch the same row.

00:02:00.581 --> 00:02:05.068
In a database with a million accounts, one account has nothing to do with the next.

00:02:05.068 --> 00:02:09.556
Out of a thousand transactions running at once, about nine hundred eighty are independent and never wait.

00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:14.044
Only ten to twenty actually conflict and need the lock manager to serialize them.

00:02:14.044 --> 00:02:18.532
Three design choices keep that cheap.

00:02:18.532 --> 00:02:23.020
The lock manager keeps a hash table, so every lock check is O of one at any scale.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.508
The WAL turns scattered updates into one sequential stream, the fastest pattern a disk has.

00:02:27.508 --> 00:02:31.995
And each component does one job behind a tiny interface.

00:02:31.995 --> 00:02:36.483
The scheduler never touches the disk.

00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:40.971
That separation of concerns is what lets a database run a million transactions per second on commodity hardware.

00:02:40.971 --> 00:02:44.512
That is the transaction manager.

00:02:44.512 --> 00:02:48.053
Five components, one coordination layer.

00:02:48.053 --> 00:02:51.594
Next we run a concrete trace of three transactions hitting shared data.

00:02:51.594 --> 00:02:55.135
Then we watch the recovery manager bring everything back after a crash.
